{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nLittle Johnny Bubbles enjoys spending hours in front of his computer playing video games. His favorite game is Bubble Strike, fast-paced bubble shooting online game for two players.Each game is set in one of the N maps, each having different terrain configuration. First phase of each game decides on which map the game will be played. The game system randomly selects three maps and shows them to the players. Each player must pick one of those three maps to be discarded. The game system then randomly selects one of the maps that were not picked by any of the players and starts the game.Johnny is deeply enthusiastic about the game and wants to spend some time studying maps, thus increasing chances to win games played on those maps. However, he also needs to do his homework, so he does not have time to study all the maps. That is why he asked himself the following question: \"What is the minimum number of maps I have to study, so that the probability to play one of those maps is at least $$$P$$$\"?Can you help Johnny find the answer for this question? You can assume Johnny's opponents do not know him, and they will randomly pick maps.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class I {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = s.nextLong();\n        long p = (long)(s.nextDouble() * 10000);\n        solve(n, p);\n    }\n\n    public static void solve(long n, long p) {\n        long studied = 0;\n        long notStudied = n;\n\n        while (true) {\n            long zero = comb3(notStudied);\n            long one = studied * comb2(notStudied);\n            long two = comb2(studied) * notStudied;\n            long three = comb3(studied);\n\n            long thisP = one + two * 2 + three * 2;\n            long total = comb3(n);\n\n            if (thisP * 5000 >= p * total) {\n                System.out.println(studied);\n                return;\n            }\n\n            studied++;\n            notStudied--;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static long comb3(long x) {\n        return x >= 3 ? x * (x - 1) * (x - 2) / 6 : 0;\n    }\n\n    private static long comb2(long x) {\n        return x >= 2 ? x * (x - 1) / 2 : 0;\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class I {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = s.nextLong();\n        double p = s.nextDouble();\n        solve(n, p);\n    }\n\n    public static void solve(long n, double p) {\n        long studied = 0;\n        long notStudied = n;\n\n        while (true) {\n            long totalWays = comb3(n);\n            \n            // Calculate favorable outcomes\n            long favorable = 0;\n            \n            // Case 1: 0 studied maps in the 3 shown\n            // Both players discard the only not-studied map shown, system picks randomly among remaining 2 not-studied\n            // Probability of playing a studied map = 0\n            long case1 = comb3(notStudied);\n            \n            // Case 2: 1 studied map in the 3 shown\n            // Players can discard maps. We need to count scenarios where studied map is selected.\n            // If both players discard the studied map: system picks from 2 not-studied -> 0 chance\n            // If one player discards studied, one discards not-studied: system picks from remaining 1 not-studied -> 0 chance\n            // If both players discard not-studied: system picks from remaining 1 studied -> 100% chance\n            // Probability both discard not-studied = (2/3)*(2/3) = 4/9? No, let's think differently.\n            // Actually, each player independently picks one of 3 maps to discard.\n            // Favorable: both players discard the 2 not-studied maps (each has 2 choices for not-studied, but they must pick different ones)\n            // Number of ways both discard not-studied: 2 * 1 = 2 (player1 picks one not-studied, player2 picks the other)\n            // Total ways: 3 * 3 = 9\n            // So probability = 2/9\n            long case2 = studied * comb2(notStudied);\n            favorable += case2 * 2; // 2 favorable discard combinations out of 9, but we count in numerator\n            \n            // Case 3: 2 studied maps in the 3 shown\n            // Favorable if at least one studied map is not discarded by either player.\n            // System picks randomly from maps not discarded by anyone.\n            // We need probability that at least one studied map survives.\n            // Total discard combinations: 9\n            // Unfavorable: both studied maps are discarded (each studied map must be discarded by at least one player)\n            // This happens when: both players discard studied maps (could be same or different)\n            // If both discard same studied: 2 ways (both pick studied1 or both pick studied2)\n            // If they discard different: 2 ways (player1 picks studied1, player2 picks studied2, or vice versa)\n            // Actually: for 2 studied maps S1,S2 and 1 not-studied N:\n            // Unfavorable: S1 and S2 both get discarded (by at least one player)\n            // This means: player1 and player2 together cover both S1 and S2 in their discards\n            // Total ways: 3*3=9\n            // Unfavorable ways: \n            // - Both pick S1: 1 way\n            // - Both pick S2: 1 way  \n            // - Player1 picks S1, Player2 picks S2: 1 way\n            // - Player1 picks S2, Player2 picks S1: 1 way\n            // Total unfavorable: 4\n            // Favorable: 9-4=5\n            // But we need to be careful: if both discard the same map, that map is discarded. If they discard different maps, both are discarded.\n            // So favorable = 5/9\n            long case3 = comb2(studied) * notStudied;\n            favorable += case3 * 5;\n            \n            // Case 4: 3 studied maps in the 3 shown\n            // All maps are studied, so whatever is played is studied\n            // Probability = 1\n            long case4 = comb3(studied);\n            favorable += case4 * 9; // 9/9 = 1\n            \n            // Actually, let me reconsider the probability calculation more carefully.\n            // The total number of outcomes is: C(n,3) * 9 (for the 9 possible discard combinations)\n            // Favorable outcomes: sum over all 3-map combinations of (number of discard combinations that result in a studied map being played)\n            \n            // Let me recalculate with correct probabilities:\n            // For each 3-map combination:\n            // - 0 studied: 0 favorable discard combinations\n            // - 1 studied: 2 favorable discard combinations (both players must discard the 2 not-studied maps, 2*1=2 ways)\n            // - 2 studied: 5 favorable discard combinations (as calculated above)\n            // - 3 studied: 9 favorable discard combinations (all result in studied map)\n            \n            long totalOutcomes = totalWays * 9;\n            double probability = (double)favorable / totalOutcomes;\n            \n            if (probability >= p) {\n                System.out.println(studied);\n                return;\n            }\n            \n            studied++;\n            notStudied--;\n        }\n    }\n\n    private static long comb3(long x) {\n        return x >= 3 ? x * (x - 1) * (x - 2) / 6 : 0;\n    }\n\n    private static long comb2(long x) {\n        return x >= 2 ? x * (x - 1) / 2 : 0;\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.5\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.6491513785440475e-06, -1.549708758830093e-05, -3.814689989667386e-06, 0.0, -1.645074735279195e-05, -1.7762025890988298e-05, -5.006777428206988e-06, -8.797258487902582e-05, -2.0265558760002023e-06, -2.145764938177308e-06, -3.7431014789035544e-05, -0.10107897222042084, -0.00013314791431184858, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -7.152555099310121e-07, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -2.145764938177308e-06, -2.861018856492592e-06, -2.145764938177308e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -1.2755313036905136e-05, -1.1920928244535389e-07, 0.0, -9.536738616588991e-07, -7.986990567587782e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -6.437280717364047e-06, -1.6331539882230572e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.005173628218472004, -5.960462772236497e-07, 0.0, 0.0, -2.622600959512056e-06, -2.50339189733495e-06, -2.861018856492592e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -8.34461570775602e-06, -0.011115886270999908, -0.0023756397422403097, -2.0265558760002023e-06, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -0.014357359148561954, -0.0017032414907589555, -0.0016180293168872595, -1.1920928244535389e-07, 0.0, 0.0, -1.3470558769768104e-05, -6.9141146923357155e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.861018856492592e-0